<Prose title="How We Review Dehumidifiers" h1="How We Review Dehumidifiers" description="The data, sources and rules behind every Enthusiast Review dehumidifier recommendation — DOE ratings, ENERGY STAR, recalls and owner reviews." dek="No lab coats, no fake test scores — just the data that actually separates a good dehumidifier from a bad one.">
  <h2>1. We start with comparable numbers</h2>
  <p>Manufacturers love big pint numbers. We compare models on their capacity under the <strong>2019 DOE test procedure</strong> (65°F, 60% relative humidity), which is the only rating that lines up across brands. Where a listing quotes the older 2012 rating (80°F), we convert or flag it.</p>
  <h2>2. We check efficiency and safety records</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>ENERGY STAR:</strong> we look up each model's Integrated Energy Factor (liters removed per kWh) — the clearest guide to running cost.</li>
    <li><strong>CPSC recalls:</strong> we search the Consumer Product Safety Commission database for every brand and model family and say so on the page when there's history.</li>
    <li><strong>Manuals:</strong> minimum operating temperature, defrost behavior, drain options, pump lift height and filter care come straight from manufacturer documentation.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>3. We read owner reviews in bulk</h2>
  <p>A dehumidifier's real weaknesses — a pump that dies after one season, a humidistat that reads ten points off, a compressor that rattles — show up in patterns across hundreds of reviews. We look for those patterns, not star averages.</p>
  <h2>4. We match the machine to the job</h2>
  <p>The best basement dehumidifier is a bad pick for a nursery. Every guide is written around one space or problem, and the picks are chosen for that job: capacity, noise, cold tolerance, drainage and footprint.</p>
  <h2>5. We keep guides current</h2>
  <p>Models get discontinued, renamed and recalled constantly. We revisit guides regularly and show the last-updated date at the top of every page.</p>
  <h2>What we don't do</h2>
  <p>We don't run a test lab, and we don't claim to. We don't accept payment for placement or let manufacturers review guides before publishing. Affiliate links help fund the site — see our <a href="/disclosure/">disclosure</a>.</p>
</Prose>
